Ladybird creates each Port synchronously, queues its early
JSON messages until the channel exists, marks it disconnected before
running onDisconnect, and never fires that event on the side that called
disconnect(). An error disconnect sets both port.error and, while the
listeners run, runtime.lastError.

Chromium returns its Port at once too, and GinPort::DisconnectHandler()
closes it without firing the local event. GinPort::PostMessageHandler()
throws an Error on a disconnected port, and serializes the
message before sending it; NativeRendererMessagingService::
DispatchOnDisconnectToListeners() scopes lastError around the event.
Ladybird matches all of that, and adds port.error.

Gecko creates the Port in Messenger::connect() before it asks
the parent process, Port::getAPI() exposes error and the receiver’s
sender, and recvPortDisconnect() sets the error before emitting
onDisconnect. Ladybird matches that API shape, with JSON in
place of structured cloning.

WebKit marks m_disconnected before the listeners run, in
WebExtensionAPIPort::fireDisconnectEventIfNeeded(), as Ladybird does.
But WebKit’s disconnect() also fires the caller’s own onDisconnect;
Ladybird doesn’t, matching Chromium and Gecko.

On a clean close, Ladybird clears a Port’s listener lists rather
than revoking them, so removeListener() and hasListener() keep
working during and after onDisconnect — as Chromium’s EventEmitter::
RemoveListener(), Gecko’s EventManager::removeListener(), and
WebKit’s WebExtensionAPIEvent::removeListener() all allow. Only a
detached context revokes them.
